 "constraints": [],
 "stem": "$ABCD$ is a parallelogram where $A({=a1}, {=a2})$, $B({=b1}, {=b2})$ and $C({=c1}, {=c2})$. Its diagonals meet at the point $M$. Find the coordinates of $M$ and then of $D$. What is the value of $x+y$ for the point $D(x, y)$?",
 "solution": [
  "$M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{AC}$: $M\\left(\\frac{({=a1})+({=c1})}{2}, \\frac{({=a2})+({=c2})}{2}\\right)=M\\left({=(a1+c1)/2}, {=(a2+c2)/2}\\right)$.",
  "$M$ is also the midpoint of $\\overline{BD}$. Let $D(x_1, y_1)$.",
  "${=(a1+c1)/2}=\\frac{({=b1})+x_1}{2}$, so $({=b1})+x_1={=a1+c1}$, hence $x_1={=d1}$.",
  "${=(a2+c2)/2}=\\frac{({=b2})+y_1}{2}$, so $({=b2})+y_1={=a2+c2}$, hence $y_1={=d2}$.",
  "So $D({=d1}, {=d2})$ and $x+y={=d1}+({=d2})={=d1+d2}$."
 ],
 "answer": "d1 + d2",
 "proposed_misconceptions": [],
 "notes": "Book example 3 twist: M may be fractional. Final answer is x+y of D so it is numeric."
}
